#pragma once

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Composite: the third pass.
//
// Upscales and blurs the reduced-resolution march back over the scene. It is
// doing two jobs, and only one of them is what it looks like.
//
// The obvious one is upsampling. The other is hiding the march's sampling
// noise -- and that is worth being explicit about, because it means the blur
// radius is currently paying for undersampling in the march rather than for
// resolution. The cost is quadratic in the radius, so anything that quiets the
// march buys more here than it does anywhere else.
//
// A BLUR CANNOT REMOVE CORRELATED NOISE. If the march's dither carries lattice
// structure, widening this radius produces a blurrier version of the same
// pattern. Atmo_RaySeed is where that gets fixed, not here.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** Depth-aware blur of the atmosphere buffer, composited over the scene.
 *
 *  Radius is in SOURCE pixels, so at half-per-axis a radius of 6 reaches twelve
 *  full-resolution pixels. The loop is bounded by a (2r+1) square but only taps
 *  inside the inscribed disc are taken -- about 79% of them.
 *
 *  CIRCULAR, NOT SQUARE. A square support reaches r*sqrt(2) into its corners,
 *  so unless the falloff has already killed those weights the kernel is longer
 *  on the diagonals than on the axes. That anisotropy prints as a hatch on any
 *  grain the blur fails to remove, and the corner taps were the least useful
 *  ones being paid for.
 *
 *  Falloff sets the Gaussian's width as a DIVISOR of the radius: sigma =
 *  radius / falloff. Tying the two fixes the SHAPE of the kernel and leaves
 *  only its size authored. A sigma set independently can leave the edge weights
 *  near 1, which is a box blur, and a box response has side lobes on the axes
 *  -- hatching for the same reason the square support does.
 *
 *  DepthTapScale is full-resolution pixels per source pixel. The colour tap
 *  moves in source texels while the depth tap moves in screen UV, so without it
 *  a tap's depth weight is read from the right direction at the wrong distance
 *  and the silhouette protection is quietly weaker than it reads.
 *
 *  BlurWeight lerps back toward the untouched centre sample, which is what
 *  makes the blur an authored amount rather than all or nothing.
 *
 *  PARAMETERS AND THE TEXTURE ID HAVE TO BE PASSED IN.
 *
 *  SceneTextureFetch is a MACRO, not a function -- it expands to
 *  SceneTextureFetchFunc(Parameters, id, offset), and both Parameters and the
 *  Tex struct are locals of the generated custom-expression function. Neither
 *  exists in an included file, so the macro cannot be used here at all.
 *
 *  Calling the underlying function directly does work, because that IS declared
 *  by the time material includes are emitted. It just needs the two locals
 *  handed to it explicitly, which is what these arguments are for. */
float4 Atmo_Composite(
	FMaterialPixelParameters Parameters,
	int SceneTextureId,
	float4 center,
	float centerDepth,
	float3 sceneColor,
	float2 screenUV,
	float falloff,
	int radius,
	float depthSharpness,
	float depthTapScale,
	float blurWeight)
{
    float4 sum = center;
    float totalWeight = 1.0;

    const float radiusF = float(max(radius, 1));

	// exp(-d^2 / 2 sigma^2), folded into one multiply. The divisor is floored so
	// a falloff of zero widens the kernel rather than dividing by it.
    const float sigma = radiusF / max(falloff, 0.25);
    const float invW = 1.0 / (2.0 * sigma * sigma);

    const float radiusSq = radiusF * radiusF;

    const float2 depthTexel = View.ViewSizeAndInvSize.zw * max(depthTapScale, 1.0);

    for (int x = -radius; x <= radius; x++)
    {
        for (int y = -radius; y <= radius; y++)
        {
            if (x == 0 && y == 0)
                continue;

            float dist2 = float(x * x + y * y);

            if (dist2 > radiusSq)
                continue;

            float2 offset = float2(x, y);
            float4 s = SceneTextureFetchFunc(Parameters, SceneTextureId, offset);

            float spatialW = exp(-dist2 * invW);

			// DEPTH WEIGHT, and it is what stops the blur bleeding atmosphere
			// across a silhouette. Normalized by the centre depth so the
			// tolerance is relative -- an absolute threshold would be far too
			// tight at planetary distance and far too loose up close.
            float2 sampleUV = screenUV + offset * depthTexel;
            float sampleDepth = CalcSceneDepth(sampleUV);
            float depthDiff = abs(centerDepth - sampleDepth) / max(centerDepth, 1.0);
            float depthW = exp(-depthDiff * depthDiff * depthSharpness);

            float w = spatialW * depthW;

            sum += s * w;
            totalWeight += w;
        }
    }

    float4 atmo = lerp(center, sum / totalWeight, saturate(blurWeight));

	// THE ALPHA IS TAKEN UNBLURRED, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
	//
	// Blurring the transmittance would soften the boundary between "atmosphere
	// in front of this pixel" and "clear", which is exactly the edge the depth
	// weight above is working to preserve. Colour can bleed; occlusion cannot.
    float centerAlpha = center.a;

    float3 finalColor = atmo.rgb + sceneColor * centerAlpha;

    return float4(finalColor, 1.0);
}
